    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d233">

                <pb facs="0467" n="405" sameAs="frus:frus1944Quebec/pg405"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the President
                        and the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d233-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, [<date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-09-13">September 13, 1944</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Top secret for the President and the Secretary from Harriman.</p>
                <p>Cable Nr 122231 WH 63 from Mr. Hull<anchor n="2" xml:id="d233-f2"/> regarding aid
                    to the insurgents in Warsaw reached me only late tonight. On the basis of an
                    earlier message received by the Military Mission from General Spaatz<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d233-f3"/> I spoke to Molotov last night regarding an
                    operation by our Air Force which Molotov approved after telephoning to Red Army
                    General Staff. Today General Deane talked to an officer of Red Army General
                    Staff inquiring what the Soviets intended to do on their own account. He replied
                    that the Red Army had nothing planned.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d233-f4"/> General
                    Deane and head of British Military Mission will jointly approach Red Army
                    General Staff tomorrow to press for the carrying out by the Red Army of the
                    promise of the Soviet Government to render assistance itself. I will see Molotov
                    if a satisfactory answer is not obtained.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d233-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d233-f1-content">
                <p>Sent to Washington by the United States Naval Attaché, Moscow, via Navy channels;
                    forwarded by the White House Map Room to Roosevelt as telegram No. <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">MR–out</hi>-401, September 14, 1944.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d233-f2" n="2" xml:id="d233-f2-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg398">p. 398</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d233-f3" n="3" xml:id="d233-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d233-f4" n="4" xml:id="d233-f4-content">
                <p>A Soviet drop of supplies on Warsaw was executed, however, on September 13. See
                    Craven and Cate, <ref target="#pg316">pp. 316</ref>–<ref target="#pg317"
                        >317</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
